The DLG Professional Bulletin Board Operating System (DLG BB/OS)
================================================================

DLG Professional is a Bulletin Board Operating System for the Amiga.  Unlike
other BBS programs, it is not one large, clunky program, but rather is a
collection of interrelated program modules that combined form a powerful
package. DLG is not a "ported" program from another, less capable, computer
system - it was designed to work the way your Amiga works.

DLG was built with both beginners and power-SysOps in mind. Although 
numbers don't tell the full story, here are some to think about:

    * up to 65000 users
    * 255 different user levels
    * 9999 different message areas
    * 9999 different file areas
    * number of lines is limited only by serial port availability

Not all of DLG is built big, though. DLG was also designed to work on even
modest Amiga setups. Here are the facts:
    
    * DLG's modular, disk based operating system design allows for 
      low memory usage
    * DLG uses from 20-100K per active bbs line depending on setup and usage
    * low CPU usage for good performance on multiple lines
    * will work with only 512K of system memory
    * works on Amiga 500, 1000, 2000, 2500, and 3000 under AmigaDOS 1.3 & 2.0
    * works with even modest Hard Drive setups.


Each DLG user can set a number of preferences for his on-line sessions:

    * help level (novice/intermediate/expert)
    * screen length and width settings to match his terminal software
    * optional MORE prompt for paced system output
    * ANSI colour, smart ANSI screen positioning, and screen clears
    * hot keys for fast single letter command input
    * preference settings for personal upload/download protocol defaults
    * choice of default settings for message editor type
    * automatic command sequence to be executed at login
    * alias name (handle)
    * create and edit a personal signature to be appended to messages and
      files
    * create list of message and file areas to search for new items

In addition, there are a number of DLG features that users find helpful:

    * online user manual and help text for individual commands
    * full user list and search capabilities
    * list of important events since last call list is kept for each user
      while he is off-line
    * ability to see who is on other BBS lines and what they are doing
    * smart menus that show only those commands the user can actually use,
      moment to moment.
    * quote of the moment module
    * today in history module

Here are some features of DLG's full online public/private mail system:

    * DLG has numerous options for editing messages:
        - free-form paragraph oriented message editor with query search and
          replace and the ability to back up over lines using a standard
          terminal program
        - powerful, full-screen free-form ANSI based message editor
        - use of an external Amiga message editor/word processor is allowed 
          from a local session.
        - support for third-party online editors

    * up to 9999 separate message areas
    * users can automatically scan the areas they choose for new messages
    * users can edit their own custom message signatures
    * message threading is supported for following conversations
    * continuous read mode with optional ansi/more-prompt disable
    * active readers of an area can be listed by any user
    * individual privileges can be assigned to each user in each area
      separately, or automatically assigned based on user level
    * smart menu displays - menus are context sensitive and choices will
      appear only when the action can be performed by the user
    * users are informed of all types of mail received
    * users can kill their own message depending on access
    * maximum number of messages allowed in each area can be set
    * messages can be renumbered at any time
    * message areas can be search based on who the messages are from, who
      they are addressed to, or on the subject line of the messages.
    * messages can be forwarded and transferred from public to private
    * messages can be re-edited by their author or by users with SysOp
      access in the message area
    * messages can be Lex checked and analyzed for readability
    * messages can be sent to online group mailing lists
    * translated message areas can be created with the help of


